### What is a dynamic Proxy?

A dynamic `Proxy` is created with `Proxy.newProxyInstance(classLoader, interfaces, handler)`. The JVM generates a class that implements all the listed interfaces, and every method call is dispatched to `handler.invoke(proxy, method, args)`. The generated class is named (e.g., `$Proxy0`), registered in the class loader, and lives as long as the loader.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.reflect.InvocationHandler;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ProxyDemo {

    interface AccountService {
        BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId);
        void credit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount);
    }

    static class RealAccountService implements AccountService {
        @Override
        public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
            return new BigDecimal("500000");
        }

        @Override
        public void credit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
            System.out.println("Credited " + amount + " to " + accountId);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountService real = new RealAccountService();

        // Dynamic proxy wrapping the real service
        AccountService proxy = (AccountService) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
                AccountService.class.getClassLoader(),
                new Class<?>[]{ AccountService.class },
                new InvocationHandler() {
                    @Override
                    public Object invoke(Object p, Method method, Object[] a)
                            throws Throwable {
                        System.out.println("[LOG] " + method.getName() + " called");
                        return method.invoke(real, a);
                    }
                }
        );

        System.out.println("Balance: " + proxy.getBalance("SAV-1001"));
        proxy.credit("SAV-1001", new BigDecimal("10000"));
        System.out.println("Proxy class: " + proxy.getClass().getName());
    }
}
```

```text
[LOG] getBalance called
Balance: 500000
[LOG] credit called
Credited 10000 to SAV-1001
Proxy class: jdk.proxy1.$Proxy0
```

### What is a hidden class in comparison?

A hidden class is created from raw bytecode via `defineHiddenClass`. It is not registered in the loader, has no usable binary name, and can be unloaded independently. It can implement any interface or even extend a class, because you supply arbitrary bytecode.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles.Lookup;

public class HiddenClassComparison {
    public void createHiddenCalculator() throws Exception {
        byte[] classBytes = generateCalculatorBytes();

        Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true,
                        Lookup.ClassOption.NESTMATE);

        Class<?> hiddenClass = hidden.lookupClass();
        System.out.println("Hidden: " + hiddenClass.isHidden());
        System.out.println("Name:   " + hiddenClass.getName());

        // Cannot be found by Class.forName
        try {
            Class.forName(hiddenClass.getName());
        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Not discoverable by name");
        }
    }

    private byte[] generateCalculatorBytes() {
        return new byte[0]; // placeholder
    }
}
```

### How do Proxy and hidden class compare side by side?

| Feature | Dynamic `Proxy` | Hidden Class |
|---|---|---|
| Creation | `Proxy.newProxyInstance()` | `defineHiddenClass()` |
| Named | Yes (`$Proxy0`) | No (contains `/`) |
| Loader-registered | Yes | No |
| Target | Interfaces only | Any class or interface |
| Bytecode generation | Automatic (from interface list) | Manual (supply bytecode) |
| Independent unloading | No (tied to loader) | Yes (default) |
| Private host access | No | Yes (with `NESTMATE`) |
| JPMS behaviour | Dynamic module | Host's module |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ComparisonSummary {
    // Use Proxy: simple interface wrapping
    // AccountService proxy = (AccountService) Proxy.newProxyInstance(...);

    // Use Hidden Class: specialized code generation
    // Lookup hidden = lookup.defineHiddenClass(bytes, true, NESTMATE);
}
```

### How does a Proxy behave under JPMS?

Under the module system, a `Proxy` for public, exported interfaces is placed in a dynamic module with the right `reads` edges. For non-public interfaces, all such interfaces must be in the same package and module, and the proxy is defined in that package.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ProxyModuleDemo {

    // Public, exported interface: proxy goes to a dynamic module
    public interface TransferService {
        BigDecimal transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransferService proxy = (TransferService) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
                TransferService.class.getClassLoader(),
                new Class<?>[]{ TransferService.class },
                (p, method, a) -> {
                    System.out.println("Proxy module: " + p.getClass().getModule());
                    return new BigDecimal("50000");
                }
        );

        proxy.transfer("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("50000"));
    }
}
```

### How does a hidden class behave under JPMS?

A hidden class is defined in the same runtime package and module as the lookup class. It inherits exactly that module's readability and access. There is no separate dynamic module, and no requirement to export or open additional packages.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles;
import java.lang.invoke.MethodHandles.Lookup;

public class HiddenClassModuleDemo {
    public void showModuleInfo() throws Exception {
        byte[] classBytes = new byte[0]; // placeholder

        Lookup hidden = MethodHandles.lookup()
                .defineHiddenClass(classBytes, true);

        Class<?> h = hidden.lookupClass();
        System.out.println("Host module:   " + HiddenClassModuleDemo.class.getModule());
        System.out.println("Hidden module: " + h.getModule());
        System.out.println("Same module:   " + (h.getModule() == HiddenClassModuleDemo.class.getModule()));
    }
}
```

### When should you use Proxy vs hidden class?

Use `Proxy` when you just need an interface-based wrapper with a single dispatch point and do not want to generate bytecode by hand. Use a hidden class when you are writing framework-level code that generates specialized classes and needs them invisible, unloadable, or able to touch a host's private state.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class DecisionGuide {

    interface LoanService {
        BigDecimal calculateEMI(BigDecimal principal, int months);
    }

    // Application code: use Proxy for simple logging wrapper
    public LoanService createLoggingProxy(LoanService real) {
        return (LoanService) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
                LoanService.class.getClassLoader(),
                new Class<?>[]{ LoanService.class },
                (proxy, method, args) -> {
                    System.out.println("[AUDIT] " + method.getName());
                    return method.invoke(real, args);
                }
        );
    }

    // Framework code: use hidden class for specialized generator
    // Lookup hidden = lookup.defineHiddenClass(bytes, true, NESTMATE);
    // hidden.lookupClass().getConstructor().newInstance();
}
```

### What about security and encapsulation?

A hidden class does not weaken module encapsulation. It shares the host module's protection domain and cannot access a package that its host module itself cannot access. A `Proxy` over interfaces from multiple modules must have all those interfaces accessible, and non-exported interfaces require being in one package.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SecurityNote {
    // Hidden class in com.bank.service module:
    // - CAN access everything com.bank.service can access
    // - CANNOT access unexported packages of other modules
    // - Protection domain = host's protection domain

    // Proxy over non-exported interface:
    // - All such interfaces must be in same package and module
    // - Proxy defined in that package
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

For frameworks migrating off `Unsafe` or needing fine lifecycle control in modular banking applications, hidden classes avoid the dynamic-module complexity that proxies introduce. Modern JDK machinery (lambda metafactory, method handle proxies) uses hidden classes internally. Application code wiring simple interface interceptors can still use `Proxy` or ByteBuddy.
